WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:03.930
Plan a campaign, test small ads, and know if you made money.

2
00:00:04.280 --> 00:00:09.450
This is the video for module four: campaigns, paid ads, measurement and growth.

3
00:00:10.700 --> 00:00:20.100
A campaign is a focused push with one objective, an offer, a start and end date, and a budget. Write a one-page brief before you spend a naira.

4
00:00:20.450 --> 00:00:27.810
Maryam in Kaduna sells school uniforms. Her objective is 60 orders between 1 and 31 August.

5
00:00:29.060 --> 00:00:33.670
Her audience is parents of primary pupils within 5 km of her shop.

6
00:00:34.920 --> 00:00:46.510
Her offer is free name embroidery before 20 August. It costs her little but parents value it. A 20% discount on a 30% margin would leave almost nothing.

7
00:00:47.760 --> 00:00:54.240
She will use WhatsApp Status and broadcast, a Facebook ad to parents nearby, and flyers at school gates.

8
00:00:55.490 --> 00:01:05.240
Keep the offer truthful. No fake countdowns or false only 2 left messages. Once customers catch one false claim, they stop trusting the rest.

9
00:01:06.490 --> 00:01:11.950
Maryam works out what success looks like before she starts. These figures are illustrative.

10
00:01:13.200 --> 00:01:25.300
She makes about 3,500 naira profit per order. That is her break-even cost per order. She wants to keep at least half, so her target is 1,750 or less.

11
00:01:26.550 --> 00:01:33.650
She puts about 40,000 into an ad over 3 weeks, and 10,000 into flyers with her WhatsApp QR code.

12
00:01:34.900 --> 00:01:42.310
That is about 50,000 naira. She asks every buyer, where did you see our offer, and labels each chat by source.

13
00:01:43.560 --> 00:01:50.790
Choose the objective that matches the action you want. For WhatsApp sellers, a click-to-WhatsApp ad opens a chat with you.

14
00:01:51.140 --> 00:01:59.370
Amaka tested about 14,000 naira over 7 days, in Port Harcourt, with two ads. The platter photo got 4 orders.

15
00:02:00.620 --> 00:02:06.450
The packing video got 9 orders, at about half the cost per order. So she switched off Ad A.

16
00:02:06.800 --> 00:02:12.770
Compare ads by cost per order, not only cost per click. Test with money you can afford to lose.

17
00:02:14.020 --> 00:02:24.790
Ngozi spent 30,000 naira on ads for her skincare website, and got 18 purchases. That is about 1,667 naira per purchase.

18
00:02:26.040 --> 00:02:34.360
Those purchases brought 216,000 naira. Return on ad spend is revenue divided by spend: 7.2.

19
00:02:35.610 --> 00:02:45.620
But revenue is not profit. Each order costs her about 7,000 in products, packaging and delivery, so her profit before ads is 5,000.

20
00:02:45.970 --> 00:02:51.830
The rule: if cost per order is below profit per order before ads, the campaign made money.

21
00:02:53.080 --> 00:03:00.080
An A/B test compares two versions that differ in one thing. Halima's salon tested two weekday offers.

22
00:03:01.330 --> 00:03:06.860
100 clients got 10% off. 100 others got a free hot-oil treatment.

23
00:03:08.110 --> 00:03:17.300
The discount brought 8 bookings, 8%. The treatment brought 14, 14%. A test with 5 clicks each tells you almost nothing.

24
00:03:18.550 --> 00:03:27.580
She repeated it, and the treatment won again. It costs about 600 naira in products, less than the discount. So it became her standard offer.

25
00:03:28.830 --> 00:03:38.020
Stay safe. Fake Meta support messages say your page will be deleted unless you click. Real notices appear inside Meta Business Suite.

26
00:03:39.270 --> 00:03:46.760
Use cost per order against break-even to decide. Raise budgets in steps, because sudden jumps can push costs up.

27
00:03:47.110 --> 00:03:51.230
And doubling orders is only good if you can still deliver on time.

28
00:03:52.480 --> 00:03:59.490
Keeping a customer is cheaper than winning one. Bisi earns about 3,000 naira profit per meal-pack order.

29
00:04:00.740 --> 00:04:04.200
A regular orders twice a month, 24 times a year.

30
00:04:05.450 --> 00:04:07.610
And stays about 1.5 years.

31
00:04:08.860 --> 00:04:19.950
So one regular is worth about 108,000 naira in profit. Good service, follow-ups, a small loyalty reward and asking for referrals all raise it.

32
00:04:21.200 --> 00:04:27.340
To remember. Write a brief with an honest offer. Test small, and compare ads by cost per order.

33
00:04:27.690 --> 00:04:32.560
Check profit, not just revenue. Change one thing at a time, and grow gradually.

34
00:04:33.810 --> 00:04:42.680
Now practise. Write your brief with a break-even cost per order, plan two ads, calculate your metrics, and run one fair A/B test.

35
00:04:43.030 --> 00:04:44.970
Continue with the lessons below.
